<!-- Status-bar icon for household-activity notifications: the app icon's mark.

     Android draws a notification small icon from its alpha channel alone and
     tints the result, so this must be a flat white-on-transparent silhouette.
     Pointing the plugin at @mipmap/ic_launcher instead would render as a solid
     white blob on Android 5+ — the launcher art is a full-colour bitmap whose
     alpha is one opaque square. That is the whole reason the same mark has to be
     drawn twice: once in colour for the home screen, once as a silhouette here.

     The mark is the launcher icon's, traced from mipmap-xxxhdpi/ic_launcher.png
     rather than redrawn, which is why the viewport is 192 and not the usual 24 —
     these are that bitmap's own pixel coordinates, so the two cannot drift apart
     through a rounding choice. Every edge is either horizontal or exactly 45
     degrees, and the two chevrons are disjoint: the 29-unit diagonal gap between
     them is the mark's most recognisable feature and the first thing that would
     close up if the silhouette were drawn by eye.

     The group scales by five sixths about the canvas centre, which insets the
     mark to roughly 19 of 24dp. A notification icon that fills its viewport
     edge to edge sits taller than the platform's own icons in the same row.

     No fillType: neither subpath has a hole, so the default winding rule is
     enough and evenOdd would only be noise. -->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="24dp"
    android:height="24dp"
    android:viewportWidth="192"
    android:viewportHeight="192">
    <group
        android:scaleX="0.83333"
        android:scaleY="0.83333"
        android:translateX="16"
        android:translateY="16">
        <path
            android:fillColor="#FFFFFFFF"
            android:pathData="M113.5,3.5 L169.5,3.5 L49.5,123.5 L21.5,95.5 Z M113.5,88.5 L169.5,88.5 L120,138 L169.5,187.5 L113.5,187.5 L64,138 Z" />
    </group>
</vector>
